The program is one Python module. Every function, every class, every line of
top level setup, in an order where nothing is used before it is defined. There
is one namespace and you are the only one writing in it, so nothing you define
can collide with anything, and nothing you define needs to be imported from
anywhere else in the program.

Put every import at the top of the module, once each. Anything that
demonstrates or exercises the program goes under if __name__ == "__main__":,
so importing the module runs nothing.

The module must be valid Python. It has to parse. Indentation is four spaces
per level and uniform across the whole file, and it is meaning, not
decoration: a function whose body is indented by one space is a broken program
even when every character in it reads correctly.

Emit no comments and no docstrings. The .human tree is the commentary.
